== A: Theorem 6.1 against the exact frontier of one block
  plan k: ask k cells in turn, stop when the needle is found; expected questions
  n_k = k - k(k-1)/(2N); if not found, the deployed system sees z and answers by the Bayes
  rule: average failure F_k = (1/N) E_z[min(D, N-k-D)], D = number of distinct cells of the
  run outside the asked ones.  Bound of Theorem 6.1(2) with m_b = 1: p (1 - 2B/N - 2 n_k/N).
  N = 2..40, B = 1..6, all k: smallest (F_k - bound) = 0.00125 at (N, B, k) = (40, 1, 0) (F = 0.02500, bound = 0.02375)
  item (3), N = B/(2 delta): least expected questions per block of the frontier (lower convex
  envelope of the plans) for average failure <= delta, against (1 - 10 delta) B/(8 delta):
    B =  5, delta = 0.05: N =   50; best plan mixture     12.22; lower bound      6.25; ratio 1.96
    B = 10, delta = 0.05: N =  100; best plan mixture     24.09; lower bound     12.50; ratio 1.93
    B =  4, delta = 0.02: N =  100; best plan mixture     24.86; lower bound     20.00; ratio 1.24
    B = 20, delta = 0.01: N = 1000; best plan mixture    247.86; lower bound    225.00; ratio 1.10
== B: additive work: all 2^K functions on K inputs, L uniform calls, good = at most m wrong
  lower bound for every protocol: failure <= delta for all members forces
  n >= K (1 - 2 (m/L) - 2 delta)   [each call at an unasked input is wrong w.p. >= 1/2 on average
  over the class, so E[wrong calls] >= L (K - n)/(2K); and E[wrong calls] <= m + delta L]
     K    L    m  delta | lower bound | best protocol | Protocol R, M^(T) | T in 2nd half | Protocol R, last | R3 bound
    20   20    2   0.10 |        12.0 |            18 |               286 |          68 |               46 |      924
    50   50    5   0.10 |        30.0 |            44 |               767 |         158 |              107 |     2888
    50  200   20   0.10 |        30.0 |            43 |               799 |         154 |              100 |     3301
   200  100   10   0.10 |       120.0 |           172 |              3136 |         606 |              395 |    12603
   200  100   10   0.01 |       156.0 |           181 |             31356 |         790 |              482 |   126027
   400 1000  100   0.05 |       280.0 |           332 |             12850 |        1244 |              726 |    54903
  (Protocol R asks one uniformly chosen call per run, which here is a uniform input; 'last' deploys
   the mixture after all n answers (nothing is proved for it in assumptions.md; the file
   research/prove-c/rehearsal-core.md announces a bound for scaffolds of this kind); 'T in 2nd half'
   deploys the mixture of a round drawn uniformly from the second half of the rounds, for which
   the proof of R1 gives the bound 2 L K ln 2/n.)
