    n =  6.9e+04 one-hour answers:      7.871 calendar years =       34.5 working years
    n =  6.9e+07 one-hour answers:       7871 calendar years =   3.45e+04 working years
  (4) sequential rounds (Proposition 6.3): with b answers per batch and S batches the bound of R3
  is K ln 2/(theta S).  Twenty batches, theta = delta = 0.1: K <= 0.289 bits, as the file says.
== G: the table of the data against the human (the referent test where R1 is empty)
  X = [N], one call per run at a uniform input, human f = 0 with weight 2^-K; for every set A
  of inputs the member 'answer 0 on A, 1 elsewhere' (the table of a data set with questions A)
  with weight proportional to (2N)^-|A|: b = log2(2N) bits per recorded question.  After s
  distinct questions, probability that the mixture answers 1 at an input not yet asked:
    N = 1024, K = 100 bits, b = 11 bits, K/b = 9.1:  s = 0: 0.9995;  s = 4: 0.9995;  s = 8: 0.9993;  s = 9: 0.6654;  s = 11: 0.0000;  s = 14: 0.0000
    N = 1024, K = 1000 bits, b = 11 bits, K/b = 90.9:  s = 0: 0.9995;  s = 45: 0.9995;  s = 81: 0.9995;  s = 90: 0.9985;  s = 101: 0.0000;  s = 137: 0.0000
    N = 1048576, K = 1000 bits, b = 21 bits, K/b = 47.6:  s = 0: 1.0000;  s = 23: 1.0000;  s = 42: 1.0000;  s = 47: 0.9999;  s = 53: 0.0000;  s = 72: 0.0000
  the bound of R1 for the same n, K ln 2/n, is below 1 only for n > K ln 2: the tables lose to
  the human only after about K/b questions, and before that the mixture is the table's.
